based on the classic novel of the same  name the neverending story is treasured  by young and old fans alike decades  after its release the film continues to  resonate with audiences but there's more  to this timeless tale than you might  realize  here's the untold truth of the  neverending story Noah Hathaway brought  an entire gamut of emotions to his  portrayal of the young warrior Atreyu  from his intense vulnerability as he  mourns the loss of his horse  artax to his fierceness and battle  against the evil Gomorrah it's hard to  imagine the film with anyone but  Hathaway in the role however his journey  to being cast was nearly as arduous as  his character's journey in the movie  talking to Entertainment Weekly for the  film's 35th anniversary Hathaway  revealed that he was among 50,000  hopefuls from around the world who  auditioned for the part of the strong  and sensitive  Atreyu when he was finally cast the  film's original director ended up  leaving the project opening the door for  Wolfgang Petersen to take over which  meant that Hathaway had to begin the  audition process all over again  in total Hathaway recalls auditioning at  least 10 times but in the end he  survived the casting process and finally  became a Trejo  [Music]  No  while Hathaway began acting long before  the neverending story  afterward he only had a few more  on-screen appearances before mostly  retiring Hathaway has also worked as a  tattoo artist and of course he was asked  to do a lot of tattoos inspired by the  neverending story as if the scene with  Atreyu and art acts and the swamps of  sadness isn't traumatic enough there was  a horrible urban legend that the real  horse didn't survive the film shoot what  don't worry  that story is completely untrue after  filming the production even gave  Hathaway one of the horses from the film  as well as the saddle for all his hard  work unfortunately it was too  complicated to ship an animal  internationally Hathaway ended up giving  the horse to his riding double  luckily the horse went on to live for  another 20 years in Germany Hathaway  continues to joke that he feels  responsible for so many people's  childhood trauma because of that  heartbreaking scene with artax Hathaway  has also mentioned that he feels like  he's giving fans a little therapy when  he meets them at his convention  appearances while no horses were hurt  during the many difficult days of  shooting the movie the same can't be  said for Hathaway  even before primary filming began one of  the horses fell on him and he ended up  with a broken back he was in traction  for an entire month and they weren't  sure if he would be healed in time to  actually play a Trejo after everything  he'd been through to land the part  losing it before even beginning would  have been heartbreaking  luckily Hathaway healed up quickly even  though he would be injured a few more  times while working on the set in  particular during the swamps of sadness  seen when a Trejo is face-to-face with  Morla the ancient one those scenes took  the production to grueling months to  film and saw Hathaway constantly soaking  wet and covered in muck for much of the  time because of his initial accident  with the horse Hathaway has titanium  screws in his spine and still has to  deal with painful back issues the  neverending story is filled with  fantastical characters who are brought  to life using practical special effects  and puppetry and as hard as it is to  conceive of the movie without Hathaway  as a Trejo  it's almost weirder to find out that the  voices of the kindly Falk or the  limestone loving rockbiter and the  menacing Gamora were all provided by the  same person after primary filming was  finished veteran character and voice  actor Alan Oppenheimer who was also  featured in such iconic shows  he-man the Smurfs and transformers was  first approached by peterson to do the  voice of Falk or Oppenheimer flew to  Germany and did his work but wasn't  happy with his first attempt as the  voice of the luckdragon  he went back for a second pass at fall  Corps and in the process  Oppenheimer completely fell in love with  the movie since Peterson had yet to find  appropriate voices for the rock fighter  and Kimura he had Oppenheimer give it a  shot  the result was three distinct characters  that you'd never guess were all voiced  by the same person on top of that  Oppenheimer  also ended up performing the voice of  the narrator we can't help but wonder  how Petersen was able to find one actor  who could contribute so much to the film  mm-hmm with look plenty of fans of the  movie have imagined what it would be  like to ride Falkor through the skies of  Fantasia or on earth chasing down some  bullies but for Hathaway the experience  left something to be desired  Falkor was actually a huge contraption  that at times was suspended 20 feet in  the air and required a minimum of 15  people to operate pathaway told  Entertainment Weekly riding Falkor  wasn't as glamorous as you might imagine  sometimes it would overheat and it would  start going out of control probably once  every 20 minutes it ended up being like  riding a bucking bronco I just had to  hold on for dear life from time to time  but it was fun in the same Entertainment  Weekly interview Petersen pinpointed a  major reason the film still resonates so  deeply even in the age of HD it took a  lot of work to have it come out the way  it did on film and I believe that's what  gives this movie such a great quality  that can be appreciated after all these  years the creatures were so real how  they had to interact with the actors it  wasn't like they were just standing in  front of this green screen and  pretending and for those who still dream  of riding Falkor you can visit him at  bavaria film studios in germany where  the actual luckdragon from the film is  part of an exhibit that's open to the  public even the most devoted neverending  story fan might not know that there are  essentially two different versions of  the soundtrack out in the world the  German version was fully scored by Klaus  doll dinger but s Petersen considered  his vision going international he  brought in songwriter and composer  Giorgio Moroder to add some final  flourishes to the score that North  Americans are most familiar with another  huge difference is that the German  version of the movie doesn't have the  famous beer worm of a theme song sung by  pop star Lomov the song ended up being a  hit  but it almost wasn't included in the  film because the then president of EMI  records absolutely hated it  however because moroder was so impressed  with lamal after meeting him he showed  support for the singer and the tune  ended up opening the movie in a huge way  the song had a major resurgence in 2019  when it was featured in an episode of  stranger things and after listening to  Dusty bun and Suzy Pudu at the iconic  tune over and over again fans kept  coming back to the same thought and want  to hear it not right now yes now Dusty  bun unlike many of its contemporaries  the neverending story has aged really  well one of the reasons is because  Peterson was an absolute perfectionist  consistently requiring multiple takes in  order to get every scene exactly right  speaking to the News Tribune Hathaway  noted that a three-month filming  schedule ended up turning into a whole  year because Peterson kept requiring  take after take however eagle-eyed  viewers of the film might notice that  some of the actors in the English  version appear to be dubbed this is  because many of them were German and  didn't speak English at all they read  their lines in German and American voice  actors were brought in later to  re-record English versions of their  scenes the terrifying threat to Fantasia  and the human world as well was a  dreadful void called the nothing  well according to gue Mork the nothing  is an emptiness or despair fueled by the  loss of hopes and dreams that nothing  was an esoteric threat but also very  real in the world of the neverending  story talking to sci-fi now the movie  special effects coordinator Brian  Johnson explained that he conceptualized  the nothing in the movie by essentially  using the horizon as a starting point  then showing the natural world  disintegrating and disappearing into it  this was one of the few moments when  camera tricks were utilized the  nothing's unique effects are depicted as  colors splashed across the sky while the  illusion of nothingness takes center  stage Peterson was no stranger to  critically acclaimed films however he  wasn't familiar with making an  english-language movie that would  connect with english-speaking audiences  through his film das boat Peterson  became friends with Steven Spielberg and  they often chatted about filmmaking over  the phone when Spielberg was in the  early stages of planning Schindler's  List Peterson helped him Scout different  locations for filming in return  Spielberg was more than happy to take a  look at Peterson's working version of  the neverending story and he helped cut  some foot  that he felt would paste the movie  better for international audiences in  particular North Americans Peterson has  said that Spielberg admitted he learned  those tips from George Lucas who helped  him develop his own sense of pacing  because of Spielberg's edits the  American version of the movie is seven  minutes shorter than the German one as  thanks for his notes and kindness  Peterson gave Spielberg one of the most  coveted props from the film the Orang  necklace that Atreyu where's Spielberg  still has the Orang to this day mikail n  does the neverending story was a global  bestseller after its 1979 publication  when the books rights were sold for the  film adaptation Peterson was supposed to  write the script for Enda himself but  this is where problems with the  production began Enda didn't understand  why his enormous story would need to be  altered in order to fit a two-hour  running time Peterson said the story was  so sacred to Enda that it made  collaborating with him extremely  difficult so Peterson ended up  co-writing the script with Herman Wagle  instead when Peterson sent over the  final draft Enda was so furious at how  it turned out that he tried to sue to  have production stopped the movie came  out anyway and unsurprisingly and I  hated it in fact he had some especially  scathing things to say about the first  gate that Atreyu passed on his way to  the southern Oracle the Sphinx's are  quite one of the biggest embarrassments  of the film they are full bosom  strippers who sit there in the desert  Enda also called the film disgusting and  even went so far as to accuse the  production of ruining his story just for  the money claiming they didn't  understand the core themes of the book  because of Enders absolute hatred of the  movie Petersen said that the rights to  the story are tied up in litigation with  no end in sight basically don't expect a  remake anytime soon Petersen is  completely okay with that and the film  he directed he told Entertainment Weekly  it is my understanding that some films  you should leave alone and that's how I  feel about this film I like the way the  film is with all its old-fashioned charm  another person who has some thoughts on  the possibility of a remake is the  childlike Empress herself Tammy Stroh  neck while Stroh neck has admitted she  would enjoy watching a remake she also  had a very positive perspective on the  message of the existing movie I think  the film is an invitation to grow the  space of making and creating hopefully  if Hollywood diversifies and there are  more women directors and more minorities  writing scripts the message of the film  will be the ultimate winner here so I  don't think when  to remake this but I think we need to  keep growing the space for everyone  ultimately a remake seems unnecessary  since the original film has more than  stood the test of time and if fans truly  want to experience more of Fantasia they  can take bastions lead find a secluded  spot and read the original neverending  story check out one of our newest videos  right here plus even more looper videos  about classic movies are coming soon  subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]

